  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 135 PM EDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nWeakening deformation rain shield remains on track to clip areas \nmainly along and east of the Interstate 69 corridor later this \nevening into Sunday morning. This light rain, possibly into the \nquarter to half inch range in northwest OH, will be associated with \nthe remnants of Isaias making the more easterly turn into eastern \nKY. Chances for measurable rain fall off into northwest IN and \nsouthwest MI as there will likely be a rather sharp cut-off to the \nprecip shield. Forcing and the primary moisture axis exit off to the \neast by later Sunday morning-afternoon. Some afternoon sunshine may \nallow highs to get well into the 70s in our western zones, while \nclouds linger through much of the day in the east with highs \nstruggling to get out of the 60s. \n\nRidging through the column settles in for Sunday night with clearing \nand diminishing winds potentially supporting fog development early \nMonday. Strong warm advection within increasing low level southwest \nflow then develops on Monday, likely pushing afternoon highs into \nthe 80s.  \n\nA low amplitude shortwave will track east through the \ncentral/northern Great Lakes, dropping a slowing cool front into the \nlocal area on Tuesday. This will bring slightly cooler air into at \nleast the northern half of the area. Overall moisture quality and \nforcing are not overly impressive resulting in relatively low (20-\n30%) chances for a few showers/storms, best chances Monday night \ninto Tuesday morning. \n\nFor the second half of the week, there remains model spread on \nwhether a stronger phased system comes together over the northern \nGreat Lakes, or just a weaker northern stream shortwave tracking \nsoutheast through the Great Lakes and the Northeast. There should be \ncold frontal passage regardless later Thursday or Friday with \nchances for rain, and a trend toward cooler temperatures in time for \nnext weekend.",
